Myth: In the beginning, Rivers flowed from the mountains to the sea in straight, hurried paths, jealously guarding every drop they carried. They dug deep, narrow channels and refused to spill onto the banks.

"Why should I give my water to the land?" River grumbled. "These drops are my treasure, my essence. The soil produces nothing for me."

The valleys beside River remained dust and stone. Nothing grew. Nothing lived.

One year, winter snows were heavy. River, flowing in its narrow channel, could not contain the sudden abundance. It burst its banks unwillingly, spilling across the parched valley floor.

River expected to feel depleted, diminished. Instead, something remarkable happened. The flooded soil sprouted thousands of seeds that had waited dormant for generations. Grasses grew, then trees, their roots holding the valley walls. When summer came and River shrank, these roots prevented erosion, keeping River's channel clear and strong.

"The plants protect me," River realized. "My overflow nourishes them, and they stabilize my path."

Now River meanders generously, creating wetlands and floodplains, understanding that water given to the land returns as strength. The valley thrives, and River flows more reliably than when it clutched every drop, having learned that generosity creates abundance for both giver and receiver.

